1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,500
The Secret Life of Bees.

2
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:11,012
A honeybee colony operates as a superorganism, its 30,000 to 60,000 members functioning as a single reproductive and foraging unit.

3
00:00:11,012 --> 00:00:19,937
The queen, the sole fertile female, lays up to 2,000 eggs daily during peak season, producing a lifetime total exceeding one million.

4
00:00:19,937 --> 00:00:43,612
Worker bees, all female, progress through age-based task specialization called temporal polyethism: cleaning cells (days 1-3), nursing brood (days 4-10), producing wax from abdominal glands and building comb (days 11-20), guarding the hive entrance (days 18-21), and finally foraging (days 22 until death at roughly six weeks).

5
00:00:43,612 --> 00:00:53,700
A single forager makes approximately 10 trips per day, visiting up to 100 flowers per trip, and produces roughly one-twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in her lifetime.

6
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,400
The colony collectively visits millions of flowers daily.

7
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:06,937
Bees communicate resource locations through the waggle dance, decoded by Karl von Frisch in 1945 (Nobel Prize 1973).

8
00:01:06,937 --> 00:01:21,575
A returning forager runs a figure-eight pattern on the vertical comb: the angle of the straight run relative to gravity indicates the direction relative to the sun, while the duration of the waggle phase signals distance — each second corresponds to approximately one kilometer.

9
00:01:21,575 --> 00:01:28,637
A hive produces 20 to 40 kilograms of honey annually from an estimated 2 million flower visits per kilogram.

10
00:01:28,637 --> 00:01:36,962
Pollination services provided by honeybees and native bees are valued at over 15 billion dollars annually in US agriculture alone.

11
00:01:36,962 --> 00:01:45,962
Crops including almonds (100 percent dependent on bee pollination), apples, blueberries, and cucumbers require insect pollination.

12
00:01:45,962 --> 00:02:03,087
Colony Collapse Disorder, first identified in 2006, has caused annual colony losses of 30 to 45 percent in the United States, driven by pesticide exposure (particularly neonicotinoids), the parasitic mite Varroa destructor, and habitat loss.

13
00:02:03,087 --> 00:02:10,912
Beekeepers globally manage approximately 92 million hives, producing 1.8 million tonnes of honey per year.

